# Security

Every rule here maps to a common class of vulnerability. Each section starts with one line on the attack it prevents. When a rule and a deadline conflict, keep the rule and raise the conflict with the user.

## 1. Secrets and configuration

Attack prevented: leaked credentials, and apps that silently run with weak defaults.

Must:
- Read config only through `AppConfigService` on the backend and `src/config/env.ts` on the frontend. Both validate env with Zod at startup.
- Add every new variable to the Zod schema and to `.env.example` with a placeholder value.
- Make secrets required in the schema. The app must refuse to start without them.
- Prefix a frontend variable with `NEXT_PUBLIC_` only when the browser needs it. Anything with that prefix is public.

Never:
- Give a secret a default value (`JWT_SECRET ?? 'dev-secret'`).
- Read `process.env` outside the config files.
- Commit `.env` files, keys, tokens or real credentials, including in tests and seed scripts.
- Put a secret in a `NEXT_PUBLIC_` variable.

## 2. Authentication

Attack prevented: stolen sessions, password cracking and account enumeration.

Must:
- Keep both tokens in httpOnly cookies, following `docs/PLAN.md` section 3.4. JavaScript never reads or stores a token.
- Hash passwords with bcrypt at cost 12, through `hashData` and `compareData`.
- Hash refresh, verification and reset tokens with SHA-256 through `hashToken` before storing them. bcrypt reads only the first 72 bytes, so it can't tell two JWTs apart.
- Check `tokenVersion`, `status` and `deletedAt` on every token validation.
- Bump `tokenVersion` on password change, password reset, suspension, soft delete and "log out everywhere".
- Return the same message for "wrong email" and "wrong password". Forgot-password returns the same response whether or not the email exists.
- Make verification and reset tokens single-use with an expiry. Clear the hash once used.

Never:
- Put a token in `localStorage`, `sessionStorage`, a non-httpOnly cookie, a URL (except one-time email links) or a log.
- Return `passwordHash`, `refreshTokenHash`, token hashes or `tokenVersion` in any response.
- Write your own JWT parsing. Use `@nestjs/jwt` and the Passport strategy.

## 3. Authorization

Attack prevented: users reading or changing data that isn't theirs (IDOR), and privilege escalation.

Must:
- Treat every route as private. The global `JwtAuthGuard` enforces that, and `@Public()` is the only opt-out.
- Put `@Roles(...)` with `Role` enum values on every admin route handler. Admin and user routes share one controller per module, so the check belongs on each method. A handler without it is open to every signed-in user.
- Take the user ID for `/me` routes from `@CurrentUser()`, never from the body, query or URL.
- Scope every query on user-owned data by owner, and pass the owner ID through `requireDefined`.
- Return 404, not 403, when a user asks for a record they don't own, so the response doesn't confirm the record exists.
- Stop admins from changing their own role or status, and stop anyone below `SUPER_ADMIN` from granting `SUPER_ADMIN`.

Never:
- Add `@Public()` to a route that reads or writes user data.
- Trust a `role`, `userId`, `status` or `ownerId` field sent by the client.
- Rely on the frontend hiding a button as the only access check.

## 4. Input validation

Attack prevented: malformed or oversized input, and mass assignment.

Must:
- Validate every body, query and param with a DTO. The global `ValidationPipe` runs with `whitelist`, `forbidNonWhitelisted` and `transform`.
- Give every string field a `@MaxLength`, every number `@Min` and `@Max`, and every array `@ArrayMaxSize`.
- Validate IDs with `@IsUUID('7')` and enums with `@IsEnum`.
- Check `sortBy` against a whitelist constant.
- Normalize emails to lowercase and trim them before lookup or storage.
- Validate on the frontend with Zod as well, for UX only. The backend is the authority.

Never:
- Pass a DTO straight into Prisma `data` without picking fields. Build `data` explicitly or with `buildPatchData`.
- Accept a JSON body larger than 1 MB, except on upload routes with their own limit.

## 5. Injection

Attack prevented: SQL injection and template injection.

Must:
- Query through the Prisma client.
- Use `$queryRaw` only as a tagged template (`` prisma.$queryRaw`... ${value}` ``), which parameterizes values.
- Render emails through EJS with `<%= %>`, which escapes HTML.

Never:
- Call `$queryRawUnsafe` or `$executeRawUnsafe` with any value that came from a request.
- Build a Prisma field name or `orderBy` key from raw user input without a whitelist.
- Use EJS `<%- %>` (unescaped) with user data.
- Pass user input to `eval`, `new Function`, `child_process` or a file path.

## 6. XSS and output

Attack prevented: script injection into pages.

Must:
- Let React escape output. Render user content as text.
- Keep the Content-Security-Policy and security headers in `next.config.ts` when editing that file.
- Use `next/link` or validated URLs for `href` values built from data. Allow only `http:`, `https:` and `mailto:` schemes.

Never:
- Use `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` with user content. If HTML rendering is unavoidable, sanitize with DOMPurify first and say why in a comment.
- Put user content into `<script>` tags. JSON-LD uses `JSON.stringify` output from trusted constants only.

## 7. CSRF and CORS

Attack prevented: another site making requests with the user's cookies.

Must:
- Keep auth cookies `SameSite=Lax`, `Secure` in production and `HttpOnly`.
- Keep `OriginGuard` on state-changing methods (`POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`, `DELETE`).
- Read allowed origins from `CORS_ORIGINS` in env, with `credentials: true`.

Never:
- Change state on a `GET` request.
- Set CORS `origin` to `*` or reflect the request origin.
- Hardcode production origins in `main.ts`.

## 8. File uploads

Attack prevented: malware uploads, storage abuse and path traversal.

Must:
- Enforce a size limit and an allowed-type list on every upload route.
- Check the real file type from its magic bytes with `file-type`. Don't trust the `mimetype` or the extension.
- Build S3 keys on the server as `<folder>/<uuidv7>.<ext>`.
- Keep the bucket private. Serve files through presigned GET URLs that expire within 1 hour.
- Re-encode images with `sharp` where the plan says so. That also strips metadata such as GPS location.

Never:
- Use the client's filename in a storage key or a file path.
- Make the bucket or objects public.

## 9. Redirects and URLs

Attack prevented: open redirects used for phishing, and server-side request forgery.

Must:
- Pass every `?next=` or `?redirect=` value through `sanitizeRedirectPath`, which allows only same-site relative paths.
- Build email links from `FRONTEND_URL` in config.

Never:
- Redirect to a full URL taken from a request.
- Fetch a URL supplied by a user from the server.

## 10. Errors and data exposure

Attack prevented: leaking internals that help an attacker.

Must:
- Use explicit `select` so responses contain only the fields the client needs.
- Throw Nest `HttpException` subclasses with plain messages. `HttpExceptionFilter` maps Prisma errors to safe messages.
- Return stack traces only when `NODE_ENV` is not `production`.
- Keep Swagger off in production unless `SWAGGER_ENABLED=true`.

Never:
- Send Prisma error messages, SQL, stack traces or file paths to the client in production.
- Return a full Prisma model to the client.

## 11. Logging

Attack prevented: secrets and personal data ending up in log storage.

Must:
- Log through the Nest `Logger`, which goes to pino with redaction.
- Add any new sensitive field name to the redaction list in the logger config.

Never:
- Log passwords, tokens, cookies, `Authorization` headers, reset links or full request bodies on auth routes.
- Log full user records. Log the user ID.

## 12. Rate limiting and abuse

Attack prevented: brute force, credential stuffing and email bombing.

Must:
- Keep the global `ThrottlerGuard`.
- Put a stricter `@Throttle` on login, register, refresh, forgot-password, reset-password and resend-verification.
- Enforce a cooldown on any endpoint that sends an email to an address.

## 13. Dependencies

Attack prevented: known vulnerabilities in packages.

Must:
- Commit lockfiles.
- Keep `npm audit --audit-level=high` passing. The pre-push hook runs it.
- Pick maintained packages with recent releases. Tell the user before adding a dependency.

Never:
- Install a package to replace a few lines of code.
- Use `--force` or `--legacy-peer-deps` without telling the user why.

## Review checklist

- [ ] No secret has a default, no `process.env` outside config, and no credentials in the diff.
- [ ] New routes are private unless marked `@Public()` for a clear reason. Every admin handler has `@Roles(...)`.
- [ ] Queries on user-owned data filter by owner through `requireDefined`.
- [ ] Every new DTO field has type, length or range limits. Sort fields are whitelisted.
- [ ] No raw SQL with request values, and no unescaped template output.
- [ ] No `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` with user content.
- [ ] Responses use `select` and contain no hashes, `tokenVersion` or internals.
- [ ] Uploads check size and magic bytes and use server-built keys.
- [ ] Redirect targets pass through `sanitizeRedirectPath`.
- [ ] Logs contain no tokens, passwords or full records.
- [ ] Sensitive endpoints have `@Throttle`.
- [ ] New dependencies were agreed with the user and `npm audit` passes.
